Stage 1 Tutorial Activity
 - Why Software Engineering Still Matters
Stage 1 | Introducing Software Technology Case Study with Python and Guided AI use
Learning goals
•	Explain why software engineering is broader than coding.
•	Identify stakeholders in a simple software problem.
•	Recognise missing requirements.
•	Critically evaluate AI-generated feature suggestions.
•	Explain why AI output should not automatically be treated as correct.
Activity 1 - Think-Pair-Share (10 minutes)
If ChatGPT or Copilot can produce a 100-line Python application very quickly, what knowledge does a software engineer still need?
1.	Requirements & domain understanding: Knowing what the system should do for real users and constraints.
2.	Quality & risk: Being able to test, debug, secure, and verify that the AI generated code is correct and safe.
3.	Design & evolution: Designing data models, architecture, and planning for maintenance, scalability, and change over time.
Activity 2 - Is This Software Engineering? (10 minutes)
Scenario A: A student writes a 50-line Python calculator.
Scenario B: A team develops a payroll system used by 5,000 employees.
Scenario C: An AI assistant generates a simple appointment application from one prompt.
Scenario	Programming?	Software engineering?	Why?
A	Yes	No	Small, low risk, no real stakeholders, no formal requirements or long term maintenance. Mostly just coding.
B	Yes	Yes	Involves requirements, security, reliability, compliance, testing, maintenance, and multiple stakeholders. Clear engineering process.
C	Yes	Only if engineered 	Code exists, but engineering requires validating requirements, checking correctness, ensuring privacy/security, and integrating with real workflows. Without that, it’s just generated code.
Activity 3 - SmartCare Problem Analysis (20 minutes)
Client statement: SmartCare Community Clinic currently uses spreadsheets and paper records to manage patients and appointments. The clinic wants new software to improve these processes.
Task 1 - Identify stakeholders
Stakeholder	What do they need?
Clinic management 	Reliable appointment history, basic operational reports, fewer errors, a simple system suitable for a small clinic.
Reception/administrative staff	Easy booking, cancellation, and rescheduling; clear appointment status; quick access to patient records; visibility of practitioner availability.
Practitioners eg. GPs	Accurate patient information, clear daily schedule, reliable appointment history, fewer double bookings
Patients	Smooth bookings experience, fewer mistakes, no duplicate bookings, clear appointment confirmations and cancellations.
Task 2 - Identify current problems
1.	Duplicate appointment bookings due to fragmented spreadsheets and manual processes.
2.	Difficulty locating patient records because data is scattered across paper and different files
3.	Limited visibility of practitioner availability, leading to poor scheduling.
4.	Lack of reliable appointment history for reporting or follow-ups
Task 3 - Ask client questions
1.	What minimum data must be stored for each patient 
2.	How do you currently define and track appointment status
3.	What specific reports do management need
4.	How should practitioner availability to be represented
5.	Do you need any access control or roles
Activity 4 - Critique an AI Response (15 minutes)
An AI assistant suggests appointment management; facial-recognition login; AI diagnosis recommendations; patient search; online payment; practitioner schedule view; insurance processing; automatic treatment-plan generation.
Suggestion	Client evidence?	In scope?	Decision
Appointment management	Yes, the core problem is the appointments and their status	Yes	Including central features of the system
Facial recognition login	No mention of advanced authentication	No	Excluding too complex and unnecessary for a small clinic
AI diagnosis recommendations	No mention of clinical decision support	No	Excluding high risk, outside scope of simple appointment system
Patient search	Yes, difficulty locating patient records	Yes	Including directly addressed locating patient records
Online payment	Not mentioned in case study	Maybe	Clarify with client, likely out of scope for first version
Practitioner schedule view	Yes, limited visibility of practitioner availability	Yes	Includes solves visibility of availability
Insurance processing	No mention of insurance workflow	No	Exclude – Not requested, adds complexity.
Treatment-plan generation	No mention of treatment planning	No	Exclude – Clinical responsibility not safe to automate
Exit question
Write one activity that a software engineer must perform and that cannot safely be delegated entirely to AI.
A software engineer must analyses and validate requirements with real stakeholders, because AI cannot reliably understand organisationl context, legal obligations, risks,, or the real world consequences of incorrect or incomplete requirements.
